<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/cybersecurity-threats-and-defences">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cybersecurity is <strong>asymmetric by construction</strong>: the attacker needs one weakness, once;
    the defender needs every weakness closed, always, on systems that keep changing. No product buys that;
    only a <strong>process</strong> does — inventory, threat-model, harden, monitor, rehearse, repeat. The
    cryptography lesson gave you the primitives (symmetric ciphers, public-key exchange, hashes); this
    lesson is about the messier truth that <strong>systems are broken around their crypto, not through
    it</strong> — through inputs, humans, defaults and dependencies — and builds the defensive discipline
    piece by piece.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five moves of a defensible system" widget="equationStep" config={secCfg} />

  <h2>Threat modelling: thinking like the attacker, first</h2>
  <p>
    Security begins before any control is chosen, with three questions. <strong>What are the assets?</strong>
    — not "all data" but the specific things whose theft, alteration or loss actually hurts (customer
    credentials, payment records, the ability to serve users). <strong>Who are the adversaries, and what do
    they want?</strong> — opportunistic bots scanning for any vulnerable machine; criminals monetising
    ransomware; insiders; competitors; states. <strong>What is the attack surface?</strong> — every input
    path an attacker can reach: login forms, APIs, file uploads, DNS, employees' inboxes, the software
    supply chain. Frameworks like STRIDE (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial
    of service, Elevation of privilege) turn this into a checklist per component. The output is a ranked
    list of doors and windows — which converts vague anxiety into an engineering backlog. The CIA triad
    (confidentiality, integrity, availability) keeps the priorities honest: a "secure" system that is down,
    or that silently corrupts its data, has failed two of three.
  </p>

  <h2>Injection: the boundary discipline</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest top-tier vulnerability class is <strong>confusing data with code</strong>. Worked example
    (the web lesson's XSS sibling): a login builds SQL by string concatenation —
    SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = 'INPUT' AND pass = '...'. A user types <strong>' OR '1'='1</strong>
    as the name. The database executes: WHERE name = '' OR '1'='1' — always true — and the attacker is
    logged in as the first account. Same category, other layers: XSS injects script into HTML (the
    web-development lesson), command injection shells out with user text, path traversal escapes a
    directory with ../. The defences are structural and boring, which is exactly why their omission is
    inexcusable: <strong>parameterised queries</strong> (the driver carries data separately from code),
    <strong>output escaping</strong> by default, allow-list validation, and least privilege so even a
    successful injection hits an account that can only read. Decades old, trivially fixed, still on every
    OWASP Top 10: security is not knowledge scarcity, it is execution discipline.
  </p>

  <h2>Passwords: hashing done right</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Never store passwords</strong> — store salted hashes. The mechanics matter. A hash is one-way,
    but guessing is cheap if verification is fast: MD5 computes billions of candidates per second on a
    modern GPU, so a leaked unsalted-MD5 table falls in minutes (identical passwords yield identical
    hashes, and precomputed "rainbow tables" cover common ones wholesale). The professional stack:
    a <strong>unique random salt per user</strong> (kills rainbow tables and identical-hash matches), a
    <strong>deliberately slow, memory-hard key-derivation function</strong> — bcrypt, scrypt or argon2 —
    tuned so one verification costs ~100 milliseconds of CPU/memory (imperceptible at login; devastating
    at scale, since each guess now costs milliseconds instead of nanoseconds), plus
    <strong>pepper</strong> (a secret not stored in the database) where operations allow. And because
    credentials leak anyway, the control that actually saves you is <strong>multi-factor
    authentication</strong>: a second, different factor (device code, biometric) means a stolen password
    alone no longer equals identity — arguably the highest-value control an organisation can deploy,
    since the large majority of breaches involve stolen or guessed credentials and the human element
    (recent Verizon DBIR reports put it near three-quarters).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "strong encryption makes us safe." Crypto is rarely broken; it is
  <em>bypassed</em> — keys stolen from configuration files, defaults left unchanged, TLS terminated at a
  compromised box. Kerckhoffs's principle (assume the attacker knows the system; secrecy lives only in
  the key) generalises: assume the attacker knows your design, your framework and your stack overflow
  answers, too.</p>

  <h2>The attack catalogue — and the matched defences</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Phishing and social engineering</strong> attack the human layer directly: a convincing fake
    login or an urgent request beats most technical walls, which is why training, reporting buttons and
    MFA are technical controls, not HR decoration. <strong>Malware and ransomware</strong> arrive through
    those channels or through unpatched software; the matched defence is the boring one —
    <strong>fast automated patching</strong> (most successful attacks exploit vulnerabilities fixed months
    earlier), least-privilege accounts, and <strong>offline, tested backups</strong> that turn "pay the
    ransom" into "restore from yesterday." <strong>Denial of service</strong> attacks availability with
    traffic floods (often botnets of hijacked devices); defences are architectural — anycast absorption,
    CDNs, rate limiting. <strong>Zero-days</strong> — flaws unknown to the vendor — cannot be patched in
    advance, so depth matters: assume any single layer (including the latest patch level) will eventually
    fail, and layer detection (logging, anomaly monitoring) behind prevention. <strong>Supply-chain
    attacks</strong> poison the dependencies themselves — modern software imports thousands of packages,
    so one compromised library poisons every product that uses it (the 2024 xz-utils backdoor attempt is
    the canonical case) — countered by dependency pinning, signed builds and reproducible builds.
    Throughout, the defender's superpower is <strong>least privilege</strong>: no human or service account
    holds more access than its job needs, so any single compromise stays small.
  </p>

  <h2>Defence in depth, and what happens after</h2>
  <p>
    Because every single control fails sometimes, mature systems layer them: hashed credentials behind
    MFA behind least privilege behind network segmentation behind encryption in transit and at rest behind
    monitored logs behind rehearsed recovery. <strong>Logging and detection</strong> deserve special
    emphasis: assume breach, and make the breach <em>visible</em> — the average organisation takes days to
    detect an intrusion, and attackers use that time to move laterally; immutable, centralised logs and
    alerting shrink it. <strong>Incident response</strong> is then a drilled procedure, not an improv:
    contain, eradicate, recover, and — critically — conduct a blameless post-mortem that fixes the system,
    not the person. <strong>Ethical hacking</strong> closes the loop: penetration testers and bug-bounty
    researchers run authorised attacks so weaknesses are found and paid for by defenders instead of
    exploited by criminals. Authorisation is the entire moral boundary of the profession: the identical
    skill, with and without a signed contract, is crime on one side of the line.
  </p>

  <h2>A breach anatomy, mapped to defences</h2>
  <p>
    Assemble the pieces on one generic incident — the shape of most real ransomware cases. Monday: an
    employee receives a plausible invoice email; the link leads to a credential-harvesting page
    (<strong>phishing</strong> — the human layer). Their password, reused from a breached forum, plus
    its absence of MFA, yields a real login (<strong>credential stuffing</strong>). The attacker finds
    the VPN account has access to a file share no one revokes (<strong>excessive privilege</strong>),
    copies data for leverage (<strong>exfiltration</strong>), and on Saturday detonates ransomware
    across the share (<strong>lateral movement + availability attack</strong>), finding the backups
    reachable from the same credentials (<strong>no offline backup separation</strong>). Now re-run the
    tape with the controls from this lesson: MFA blocks step two outright; least privilege limits the
    blast radius; phishing-resistant training and a report button shorten step one; segmented networks
    and immutable, offline backups turn the detonation into a restore-from-yesterday; centralised logs
    cut the dwell time; and the incident-response drill means Saturday is a procedure, not a panic.
    Note the pattern: <strong>no single control stops the chain — the defences cut its links</strong>,
    and the attacker's chain is only as strong as its weakest necessary step. That is defence in depth
    as a mechanism rather than a slogan, and it is why the boring controls (patching, MFA, backups,
    least privilege) dominate every serious breach report's recommendations.
  </p>

  <h2>When cryptography is the weak point: a misuse catalogue</h2>
  <p>
    The primitives hold; the wiring breaks. Four canonical ways. <strong>Wrong mode</strong>: AES-ECB
    encrypts identical blocks to identical ciphertext — the famous test image still shows a penguin
    through the "encryption," because ECB preserves patterns; the fix (a randomised mode — CBC with a
    fresh IV, or GCM) is one parameter away and routinely missed. <strong>Nonce reuse</strong>: GCM and
    stream ciphers XOR plaintext with a keystream derived from a nonce — reuse the nonce with two
    messages and the keystream cancels, leaking the XOR of the plaintexts; the RNG that generated
    nonces matters as much as the cipher. <strong>Homemade constructions</strong>: "I'll XOR with a
    secret key twice for safety" — combinatorial intuition fails at crypto design; the field's rule
    (use reviewed, standard constructions and libraries; never design your own) is empirical, earned
    by decades of broken improvisations. <strong>Side channels</strong>: the algorithm is provably
    secure, but its <em>timing</em> leaks — a comparison loop that returns early on the first mismatched
    character lets an attacker measure their way to a valid token character by character; the fix
    (constant-time comparison) is now standard library practice, and the lesson is general: the
    implementation, not the mathematics, is the attack surface. Together these justify the field's
    standing advice — attackers bypass crypto; make the bypass paths expensive.
  </p>

  <h2>Threat modelling, worked on a small system</h2>
  <p>
    Make the opening method concrete with the system this ladder's own atlas-style site implies: a web
    app with accounts. <strong>Assets</strong>: user passwords and personal data (breach = regulatory and
    reputational loss), session validity (account takeover), availability (downtime = lost trust).
    <strong>Adversaries</strong>: opportunistic credential stuffers (bulk, automated), a targeted grudge
    user (patient, knows the product), scrapers (want the data, not the accounts). <strong>Surfaces,
    walked with STRIDE</strong>: the login form (spoofing — credential attacks; answered by MFA and
    rate limiting); the file-upload endpoint (tampering — a "photo" that is a webshell; answered by
    type validation and serving uploads from a separate origin); the password-reset email (spoofing —
    account recovery is the side door; answered by expiring, single-use, entropy-heavy tokens); the
    admin panel (elevation of privilege — answered by role checks <em>server-side</em>, never hidden
    buttons alone); the public API (information disclosure — answered by authorisation on every object
    access, the broken-access-control class that tops modern vulnerability lists); the server itself
    (denial of service — answered by rate limits and CDN absorption). Output: a ranked backlog, each row
    a named adversary, surface, failure, and control. Twenty minutes of this exercise finds the
    misconfigurations that breach reports find every month — because most breaches are not exotic;
    they are unmodelled.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The economics explain most of the field.</strong> Attackers optimise effort per victim, so
    they mass-exploit what is common and unpatched; defenders who patch fast, delete whole attack classes
    (parameterised queries, MFA) and refuse to be the lowest-hanging fruit are, in practice, safe from
    most adversaries — security is not about being invulnerable, it is about being expensive relative to
    the attacker's return. Threat modelling formalises further: attack trees assign costs to paths, and
    the defender's job is to make every cheap path expensive. <strong>Formal ground truth</strong> exists
    only in cryptography and proofs: memory-safe languages (Rust) eliminate whole bug classes by
    construction; formally verified crypto libraries exist; but the human-protocol layer — phishing,
    fraud, coercion — has no patch. <strong>Open problems</strong>: quantum computers threaten RSA and
    elliptic-curve signatures ("harvest now, decrypt later" makes the transition urgent for long-lived
    secrets — post-quantum standards are already published and migrating); AI-generated phishing removes
    the typos that made scams detectable; the software supply chain has millions of maintainers and no
    audit model; and IoT devices ship insecure by default at planetary scale. The discipline's honest
    summary for a new engineer: the mathematics mostly works; the systems, defaults, dependencies and
    humans are where the breaches live — so inventory your assets, threat-model the paths, never trust
    input, layer your defences, and rehearse the day one of them fails.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OWASP Top 10; NIST Cybersecurity Framework; Verizon Data Breach Investigations Report; Kerckhoffs (1883); CISA guidance on MFA and patching.</p>

